WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.840
Sadly, not every actor who joins the Marvel
Cinematic Universe ends up sticking around.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.000
Some become unhappy with Marvel, some fall
out of love with blockbusters altogether,

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.960
and some become victims of scheduling conflicts.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.620
These are the reasons some of the MCU’s
key players have said farewell to that universe.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:24.359
A lot of fans were surprised when Marvel Studios
announced in 2010 that it wouldn't ask Incredible

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.480
Hulk star Edward Norton to reprise his role
in Avengers.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.280
The company put out a press release saying,
in part:

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.010
"Our decision is definitely not one based
on monetary factors, but instead rooted in

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.890
the need for an actor who embodies the creativity
and collaborative spirit of our other talented

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:40.880
cast members."

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.460
Norton's agent called the statement "offensive"
and claimed the studio unexpectedly cut off

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.340
negotiations.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:52.100
Norton changed his story over the years, telling
NPR in 2014 that he chose to leave the role

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.540
because he wanted more diversity in his career.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:00:59.940
"What's the problem?"

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.640
Before 2010 there was already bad blood between
Norton and Marvel.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.060
Norton signed on to the lead in The Incredible
Hulk under the condition that he could rewrite

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.240
Zak Penn's script.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.770
Marvel agreed and while Norton's added scenes
were filmed, most were cut before release.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.920
Two months before the movie’s premiere,
Entertainment Weekly reported Norton was refusing

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.140
to do press for the film.

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:24.340
And the break with Marvel still appears to
be on Norton's mind.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:29.979
In July 2018 - just over a decade after Incredible
Hulk's release - he took a swipe at Marvel

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:34.369
during Comedy Central's Roast of Bruce Willis,
using his turn at the podium to say:

00:01:34.369 --> 00:01:35.650
"I tried to be like [Willis].

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.280
I did a big action movie called The Incredible
Hulk.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:39.660
You know what went wrong?

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.240
I wanted a better script."

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.900
Tim Roth, who played The Incredible Hulk’s
main villain Emil Blonsky, has revealed in

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.160
the past that Marvel actually considered bringing
him back for the sequel to The Avengers.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.540
He told Crave Online:

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:53.920
"They were going to do it.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.700
They were thinking, in The Avengers 2 or something
[…] It just kind of got swept under the

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.829
carpet I guess."

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:02.790
It isn't clear from what Roth said whether
they were considering Abomination as the main

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.240
villain, or if he would have been working
with Ultron.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:10.110
Roth has since said he isn't confident about
returning to the MCU, and considering the

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.480
unlikelihood of a Hulk sequel, his doubts
are understandable.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.520
Answering fan questions on Reddit in 2017,
Roth wrote:

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.980
"I don't think Marvel will ever use me again,
but it would be fun."

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.300
Luckily, if Roth were to reprise the role,
the fact that it’s been so long since he

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.740
last played Blonsky would actually help to
serve his character.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.160
Roth was in his late 40s when The Incredible
Hulk hit the big screen, and the fact that

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:38.320
his character was an expert soldier in an
aging body was one of Blonsky's prime motivations

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.140
for allowing himself to be transformed into
a monster.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:46.650
According to Terrence Howard, the sole reason
he didn't get to reprise his role as James

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:50.080
Rhodes in Iron Man 2 and beyond is simple
- money.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.980
Howard discussed his side of things in 2013
with Andy Cohen on Watch What Happens Live,

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.160
alleging that Robert Downey Jr. was partially
responsible.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.560
"It turns out that the person that I helped
become Iron Man… took the money that was

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.680
supposed to go to me and pushed me out."

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.500
Howard said Marvel signed him up for three
films and then, when it was time to make Iron

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:18.410
Man 2, they demanded he take an almost 90
percent pay cut.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:24.260
“And I called my friend, that I helped get
the first job, and he didn’t call me back

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.320
for three months.”

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:32.200
In a 2015 interview with Rolling Stone, Howard
clarified that he was cast before Downey and

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.370
that he took a $1 million pay cut to help
convince Downey to jump on board - though

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.460
Marvel took issue with Howard's story.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:43.680
During a second appearance on Watch What Happens
Live in 2017, the actor said fences had been

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.060
mended between him and Downey - but also that
Marvel threatened him with legal action after

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.120
his public attacks on Downey and Marvel.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.320
“Am I gonna come back and be War Machine?

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.640
I think they could have a huge franchise off
of it, but f--- ‘em.”

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:05.980
When Entertainment Weekly asked Chris Hemsworth
and Kevin Feige about Jane Foster's absence

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:11.020
in 2017's Thor: Ragnarok, their answers focused
largely on the film’s narrative.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:15.380
Hemsworth explained that Thor and Foster had
broken up and that his character was enjoying

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.150
being "a solo cowboy."

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.780
“Sorry to hear that Jane dumped you.”

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:20.840
“She didn’t dump me.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:22.720
I dumped her.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:23.900
It was a mutual dumping.”

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.980
Feige added that with Tessa Thompson's Valkyrie,
the filmmakers were matching Thor with a warrior

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:33.040
who was, quote, "much more his equal and in
many ways his superior."

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:37.479
But a much earlier story suggested there was
a real-world reason for the breakup.

00:04:37.479 --> 00:04:42.169
According to a report from THR, bad blood
started flowing between Portman and Marvel

00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:47.700
as early as 2011 over the firing of director
Patty Jenkins from Thor: The Dark World.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:52.800
The report, citing unnamed sources, said Portman
was on the fence about participating in the

00:04:52.801 --> 00:04:56.860
Thor sequel because she wanted to spend more
time with her son, who was only six months

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:57.680
old at the time.

00:04:57.889 --> 00:05:01.780
The fact that Jenkins' involvement in Thor:
The Dark World would make her the first woman

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.960
to direct such a big superhero film was what
convinced Portman to sign on.

00:05:06.189 --> 00:05:11.380
THR's sources said once Jenkins was fired,
Portman was livid with Marvel, but contractually

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:12.720
obligated to continue.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.060
Whether or not the story is true, publicly
Portman has said she's open to reprising her

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:18.280
role as Jane Foster.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:23.199
Talking to ScreenRant in early 2018, she was
asked about the possibility and said:

00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:25.009
"I'm completely open to everything.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:26.680
But I have no news about that."

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:34.140
While 2017's Thor: Ragnarok was unquestionably
a hit, one event in the film left fans furious.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:39.260
When Hela arrives on Asgard, she immediately
murders two of the famous Warriors Three:

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.120
Volstagg and Fandral.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.240
“Who are you?

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.280
What have you done with-”

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.830
Hogun the Grim at least gets a few lines of
dialogue and a bit of a tussle with Hela before

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:53.780
she kills him.

00:05:53.969 --> 00:05:59.759
Kevin Feige addressed the reason why Ray Stevenson,
Zachary Levi and Tadanobu Asano won't be seen

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:04.379
again in the MCU in a November 2017 junket
press conference.

00:06:04.379 --> 00:06:08.470
Feige said the filmmakers wanted to make sure
the audience saw Hela as a real threat.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:09.260
He explained:

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.580
"[The Warriors Three] had noble ends, mainly
to serve the arrival of Hela.”

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:18.460
The following month at the Heroes and Villains
Fan fest in San Jose, California, Levi admitted

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:23.120
to fans that he was disappointed not only
with leaving the MCU, but that Fandral's final

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.220
words were left on the cutting room floor.

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:30.300
According to Levi, the way the scene was originally
shot, the swashbuckling ladies' man charged

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.040
Hela crying "For Asgard!" before dying.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:39.020
While the Red Skull does appear in Avengers:
Infinity War, that wasn't actually Hugo Weaving

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:43.810
under all those prosthetics - it was The Walking
Dead’s Ross Marquand who replaced Weaving

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:45.260
for the third Avengers film.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.599
While Weaving had signed a multi-picture deal
with Marvel Studios, he decided he didn't

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:53.990
want to return after Captain America: The
First Avenger, and told Collider he wasn't

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:57.200
concerned about Marvel muscling him into fulfilling
his contract.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:57.980
He said:

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:01.740
"I would be obliged to, if they forced me
to, but they wouldn't want to force someone

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.460
to do it, if they didn't want to."

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:08.020
Unlike Terrence Howard and Natalie Portman,
there aren't any reports of Weaving harboring

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:12.129
any harsh feelings toward Marvel; rather,
he simply wanted to turn his attention away

00:07:12.129 --> 00:07:13.430
from blockbusters.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:14.430
Weaving told Collider:

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.920
"I think I've done my dash with that sort
of film.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.219
It was good to do it and try it out, but to
be honest, it's not the sort of film I seek

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:22.700
out and am excited by."

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:27.060
For a long time after Captain America: The
First Avenger, the only blockbuster films

00:07:27.069 --> 00:07:31.840
Weaving had been a part of was his return
as Elrond in the first and third Hobbit movies,

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.860
and even in those films his screen time was
relatively short.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:39.960
While you'd be hard-pressed to find many fans
complaining about Josh Brolin's casting as

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:43.620
Thanos, he wasn't actually the first actor
to play the Mad Titan.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:49.039
Brolin first played Thanos in 2014's Guardians
of the Galaxy, but it wasn't Brolin giving

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:53.919
us that sinister profile in the mid-credits
scene of The Avengers - that was Damion Poitier

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.300
, best known as Duprez in HBO's True Blood.

00:07:57.449 --> 00:08:01.680
Poitier told Geeks WorldWide that he didn't
know he'd been cast as Thanos until he was

00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:03.080
sitting in the make-up chair.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:03.660
He said:

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:04.740
"I thought I was Kl'rt.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:08.500
I thought I was going to be Super Skrull,
because the whole rumor was that the Skrulls

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:10.120
were going to be in Avengers."

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:14.749
No reports suggest that Marvel was in any
way unhappy with Poitier's performance.

00:08:14.749 --> 00:08:19.199
More likely it wasn't a question of Poitier
being the wrong choice, but of Brolin being

00:08:19.199 --> 00:08:20.199
so right.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.860
Kevin Feige has told Collider:

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:26.860
"You look at [Brolin's] face and the performance
he gives, he could be Thanos without any effects.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:30.169
He has that kind of face and that kind of
gravitas to it."

00:08:30.169 --> 00:08:34.850
Though he never reprised his role as Thanos,
The Avengers wasn't Poitier's last appearance

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:35.850
in the MCU.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:40.500
He also had a small speaking role in Captain
America: Civil War as one of Crossbones' henchmen.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:42.680
“Drop it.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:44.540
Or I’ll drop this.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.060
Drop it!”

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:56.400
Sadly, Aaron Taylor-Johnson's Quicksilver
didn't survive 2015's Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:01.840
“You didn’t see that coming?”

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:10.080
His death came as a surprise to many fans,
who were quick to speculate that the motivation

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.470
to kill the character had something to do
with Evan Peters being introduced as Quicksilver

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:20.470
in the previous year's X-Men: Days of Future
Past - long before Disney acquired Fox.

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:26.090
Speaking to MTV in May 2015, Kevin Feige said
Fox's Quicksilver had nothing to do with the

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:26.580
decision.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:27.280
He said:

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:31.680
"[Quicksilver's death] adds stakes to the
ending of the film… to show repercussions

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:36.620
to Ultron's actions, and also in a way to
solidify Scarlet Witch's character, and Wanda's

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:39.840
arc in the movie and where we'll see her in
the next films."

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.310
Feige went on to say Quicksilver died in "every
draft" of the script.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:50.150
In 2016, while promoting another movie, Taylor-Johnson
echoed Feige and said Age of Ultron writer/director

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:54.580
Joss Whedon was "lulling the audience into
a false sense of security," and playing with

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:58.280
the widespread fan belief that it would be
Hawkeye who didn't survive the film.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:02.440
But Taylor-Johnson had a slightly different
story to tell while talking to CinemaBlend

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:03.820
in 2015.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.840
While he did say the plan always was for Quicksilver
to die, he also admitted there was a costume

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:13.760
designed for him if his character were to
survive - and that it was, in his words, "really

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:14.260
cool."

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:15.180
Oh well!

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:19.460
"Keep up old man!”

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:21.460
“Nobody would know.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:23.240
Nobody."

